\section*{Uniform Gamma from tracial-ultrapower real rank zero}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The paper claims uniform Gamma from real rank zero in the tracial-completion ultrapower for simple unital nuclear stably finite algebras. Comparison gives absorption, also under specified bounded-trace hypotheses in the stably projectionless setting. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Nuclear averaging and scalar block compressions preserve coefficient traces while packing small projections with controlled commutators. A separate comparison construction rounds ranks, forms clipped sums of centered symmetries, and extracts Gaussian trace partitions. In the projectionless case, a combined overlap-and-model cost controls small splittings before reindexing amplifies them to exact central partitions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 291, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection gives uniform Gamma from real rank zero of the designated tracial ultrapower. The documented comparison scope also treats Jiang--Su absorption and Gamma under strict comparison, with additional trace and target-rank conditions in the projectionless case. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: UniformGamma.lean. Declarations: OAI.ComparatorModel.CurrentMain.main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

